 ( { An idea closely linked to the previous theme is the exposure of the enemy king to future checks. Often it is managed by a pawn advance, as we already saw in Hellsten-Iturry ( } 15... Qf3 15. f4 { ). Here are a few more examples. } ) 
 
 *

[Event "Mastering Endgame Strategy"]
[Site "https://www.chessable.com/variation/12630171/"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "40.2"]
[White "5.5 Exposing The Enemy King"]
[Black "Example 323- Jansa- V. vs. Korchnoi- V.- Nice Olympiad 1974"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "6k1/5p2/8/1P3QP1/8/2q4p/5P1K/8 w - - 0 1"]

 { White is about to win a second pawn, but some accuracy is still required. } 1. { -KEY- } g6 $1 { Exposing the enemy king. } ( { Instead, the game went } 1. Qf6 $2 Qc7 $1 2. Kxh3 ( { or } 2. f4 Qc2 3. Kxh3 Qb3 { and the b-pawn falls anyway } ) 
Qd7 3. Kh4 Qxb5 4. g6 Qb4 $1 { (before taking on g6, Black avoids a queen exchange) } 5. Kg3 Qb3 6. f3 fxg6 7. Qxg6 Kf8 { and a draw was soon agreed. } ) 
 ( 1. b6 Qc6 $1 { isn't constructive either for White. } ) 
 ( 1. f3 { is tempting in order to keep all the pawns defended, but after } Qc4 $1 { , heading for f1 without letting White interfere by 2.Qd3, Black seems to hold. For instance, } 2. Qxh3 Qxb5 { or } 2. Kxh3 Qf1 3. Kg4 Qg1 $1 4. Kf4 Qc1 5. Ke5 Qc5 6. Ke4 Qc2 { etc. } ) 
fxg6 ( { After } 1... Qc7 2. Kxh3 Qc3 3. Kg2 fxg6 4. Qxg6 Kf8 5. Qh6 $1 { White soon forces a queen exchange, just as in the main line. } ) 
2. Qxg6+ Kf8 ( { If } 2... Kh8 { then } 3. Qh6 Kg8 4. Qg5 Kh8 5. Qh4 Kg8 6. Qg3 ) 
3. Qd6+ Kf7 4. Qf4+ $1 Ke8 ( { Or } 4... Kg6 5. Qg3 ) 
5. Qe3+ Qxe3 6. fxe3 { White wins - Jansa. } 
