 { In this tense position Black has the more advanced passed pawn, but even so he is in trouble due to his unsafe king and the fact that White is to move. } 1. { -KEY- } Qg8+ $1 { Before placing his queen on the strong c4-square, Nikolic brings out the enemy king so as to expose it to future checks. } ( { a) Instead, } 1. b6 $2 d2 { only leads to a draw. } ) 
 ( { b) While the immediate } 1. Qc4 $6 { is also unconvincing in view of } Kg7 $1 2. b6 Qb2 ) 
Kf5 2. Qc4 $1 { A typical pin in queen endings. Now it is difficult for Black to find a good move. } Qc2 ( { After } 2... Kg6 $2 3. b6 { Black can no longer play } Qb2 { due to } 4. Qxd3 { with check } ) 
 ( { while the pawn ending that emerges after } 2... Qe4 3. Qxe4 Kxe4 { is won for White thanks to his outside passed pawn: } 4. Kf1 $1 Kd4 5. Ke1 Kc5 ( { or } 5... Kc3 6. Kd1 ) 
6. Kd2 Kxb5 7. Kxd3 h5 ( { if } 7... Kc5 { then } 8. Ke4 Kd6 9. Kf5 Ke7 10. Kg6 { etc } ) 
8. Ke4 Kc4 9. f4 $1 { followed by Kf5 - L.B.Hansen; } ( { but not } 8... Qc2 8. Kf5 $2 Kd3 $1 { with a draw soon. } ) 
 ) 
3. Qd5+ Kg6 4. b6 Qb1 ( { The desirable } 4... d2 { fails to } 5. Qg8 $1 Kh5 ( { or } 5... Kf5 6. Qh7 ) 
6. Kh3 $1 { with decisive mating threats - Seirawan; e.g. } Qg6 7. g4 Kg5 8. Qd5 f5 9. Qxd2 { etc. } ) 
5. b7 d2 6. Qg8+ $1 { The story is repeated. } Kh5 ( { Now } 6... Kf5 7. Qh7 { costs Black the queen, so he has to expose his king to a mating attack. } ) 
7. Qf7+ ( { Instead } 7. Qe8 Kg5 8. f4 $6 { allows } Kf5 { somewhat prolongs the battle since White, contrary to the game, doesn't have a skewer with 9.Qh7+ at their disposal. } ) 
Kg5 ( { In the event of } 7... Qg6 { White wins by } 8. g4 Kg5 9. f4 $1 Kxf4 10. b8=Q ) 
8. f4+ $1 Kg4 9. Qe6+ Kh5 { Other replies lead to mate in one. } 10. b8=Q $1 ( { In view of } 10. b8=Q $11 d1=Q $11 ( { or } 10. b8=Q Qxb8 11. Qf5 ) 
11. Qh3 Kg6 12. Qg8 { Black resigned. } ) 

 1-0
